<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herod Agrippa I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 11 BCE–44 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Agrippa I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herod Agrippa I was a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a>, raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and a friend of the emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>. Caligula gave him the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrarchy">Tetrarchy</a> of Philip with the title of king in 37, and by 41 Claudius had added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, reuniting his grandfather's realm. He died suddenly at Caesarea in 44, and Judaea returned to direct Roman rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i.jpg" alt="Herod Agrippa I" width="320" height="291" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adaptation of CNG by Mogador · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piece_Agrippa_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Herod Agrippa was born around 11 BCE, the son of Aristobulus IV, a child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> and Mariamne the Hasmonean. His mother Berenice was a daughter of Salome, sister of Herod the Great, so he descended from both the Hasmonean and Herodian dynasties. His parents named him after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa">Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa</a>, a close collaborator of Emperor Augustus. He spent his childhood and youth at the imperial court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where he became a friend of the princes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> and Drusus.</p><p>After the death of Drusus he fell into disgrace and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>, having squandered much of his fortune on the favour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>. Around 26 he married his cousin Cypros, and they lived modestly in a fortress at Malatha in Idumea. Their son became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agrippa-ii">Herod Agrippa II</a>. Pursued for debts, he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, borrowed money there, and sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the spring of 36.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In Rome Tiberius made him guardian of his grandson Tiberius Gemellus, but Agrippa courted the other designated heir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a>. When Caligula took the throne, Agrippa received the old tetrarchies of Philip and Lysanias, with the title of king over Batanea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trachonitis">Trachonitis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaulanitis">Gaulanitis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hauran">Auranitis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banias">Paneas</a> and Iturea. He travelled to his territories in the summer of 38, passing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> during a period of anti-Jewish feeling in which he was mocked in a street masquerade. In 40 he gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peraea">Perea</a> after the disgrace of his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas">Herod Antipas</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> was assassinated on 24 January 41. Agrippa's support and manoeuvres seem to have been decisive in raising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> to power, and he was rewarded with Idumea, Judea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, so that he ruled a realm as large as that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a>. He interceded with Claudius for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, and edicts confirmed the privileges of the Alexandrian Jews and extended them across the diaspora. His regional ambitions drew the opposition of Gaius Vibius Marsus, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, who halted the fortification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> on the emperor's orders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Agrippa died unexpectedly in 44, after about three years as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>. The cause is unknown and rumours of poisoning circulated; some researchers think <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> worried by his ambitions may have been responsible. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a> tells that after a delegation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> called his answer the voice of a god, an angel struck him down and worms devoured him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> did not give the kingdom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agrippa-ii">Agrippa II</a> but made it a Roman province under the procurator Cuspius Fadus.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> describes him as gentle and generous, and rabbinical sources present him as pious. The pagan inhabitants of Caesarea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Sebaste</a> rejoiced at his death. For many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> the end of his rule closed hopes of even a symbolic independence, and messianic, anti-Roman movements appeared afterwards.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herod Agrippa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 41 Claudius gave it with the title king to Herod, brother of <b>Agrippa I</b>, now known as Herod of Chalcis.</blockquote><a href="/state/herodian-kingdom-of-chalcis">Herodian Kingdom of Chalcis</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Herod Agrippa I</b>, grandson of Herod the Great and friend of Caligula, received the former Tetrarchy of Philip with the title king in 37.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-herod-agrippa">Kingdom of Herod Agrippa</a></li><li><blockquote>Accused of conspiracy by his nephew <b>Agrippa</b>, he was deposed by Caligula in 39 and exiled to Gaul.</blockquote><a href="/state/tetrarchy-of-galilee-and-peraea">Tetrarchy of Galilee and Peraea</a></li><li><blockquote>In 37 Caligula granted it to <b>Herod Agrippa I</b>, making it the core of the Kingdom of Herod Agrippa.</blockquote><a href="/state/tetrarchy-of-philip">Tetrarchy of Philip</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Caesarea Maritima, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.5,34.89&amp;year=24" aria-label="Caesarea Maritima on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5446 982 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Herod Agrippa I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5896 1252h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5896 1252h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=32.5,34.89&amp;year=24">What was here in 24 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
